# For those wanting to move from v1 to v2, this post is for you.

I've seen a lot of people talk about wanting to move from v1 to v2 but they don't know how.  

I have a multiple things I want to go over that will help you 

Everyone should understand that v2 was designed over a period of a decade. v2 isn't just about making AHK more object oriented, it's also about addressing all the flaws and shortcomings of v1.

Some treat v2 like it's this super-complex language now, and it's not.  
I would argue it's simpler and easier to use but requires a little more learning.  
But at the core, v2 is not THAT much different from v1.  
There is no massive learning curve to get from v1 to v2. You just have to understand a few new concepts and rules.  
It's like when you learned v1. There were rules you just HAD to learn. And you did out of necessity.  
V2 is the same way. Some rules are different. The structure is a bit different from v1. But a lot of what you did in v1 you're going to be doing in v2.  
If you're smart enough to learn v1, I **guarantee** you're smart enough to learn v2.  

This post is about educating people on the the differences between v1 and v2, how to transition to the latter, how to convert scripts, and provides a hub for people to ask v1 > v2 questions.

Topics:  

* The online docs are designed to help you convert between v1 and v2.
* No more dual syntax. Less ambiguity.
* Hotkeys and hotstrings use curly braces because they are functions.
* No global labels or gosub.
* Utilize Visual Studio + THQBY's AHK v2 addon!!
* Tips on converting code.  
* Errors are a good thing. Stop thinking they're bad.
* Callbacks are too confusing.
* Let's talk about the new classes!

# - The online docs are designed to help you convert between v1 and v2.

One of the big changes from v1 to v2 is that v2 doesn't use commands anymore. Everything is a function.  
When the commands were converted to functions, most kept their same name. It's basically the same thing except write everything like a function.  
`MsgBox` is still `MsgBox()`  
`MouseGetPos` is still `MouseGetPos()`  

There are a handful of commands that were renamed to make them more logical.  
Such as changing v1's `URLDownloadToFile` to `Download()` in v2.  

And V1 commands with subcommands, like `WinGet`, have been changed into individualized functions.  
V1 `WinGet, List` is now `WinGetList()` and `WinGet, MinMax` is now `WinGetMinMax()`.

It's pretty logical, right?  
But how are you supposed to *know* if there's a name change?  

The online docs will show you.  
Look up your v1 command using the [online v1 docs](https://www.autohotkey.com/docs/v1/).  
At the top of the docs page are [some buttons](https://i.imgur.com/B0NklaE.png).  
The one in the image marked `A` is the version button.  
This will allow you to switch between v1 and v2 equivalent pages.  
This works v1 > v2 and v2 > v1.  

Knowing about this feature of the docs is usually enough to convert basic scripts from v1 to v2.  
It's just `Look up v1 command > click v2 version > change stuff (if needed) > go to next line`  
The logic of the program shouldn't change.  
You just need to follow the slightly updated rules.

Bonus: In the picture from earlier, I marked a `B`.  
That's the settings button for the site and it goes overlooked by many.  
In there, you can set defaults for the site, including the all-important "dark mode" setting.  
(Seriously, dark mode is better for your eyes.)

# - No more dual syntax. Less ambiguity.

Some people might not understand what dual syntax means.  
Syntax means rules for something. In programming, writing something the correct way is using "correct syntax".  
Have you ever wondered why sometimes in v1 you have to use quotes around some strings and not others?  
Or why the meaning of percent signs change?  
Or why we have multiple ways to assign a value?  

It's because v1 has 2 types of syntax: Expression syntax and Classic syntax (aka legacy syntax).  
v1 commands use a mix of both types and that is CONFUSING.  
In v2, only expression syntax is used.

v1 has two ways to assign values `=` and `:=`.  
And depending on which one you use, you have to use different syntax.  
Two different ways to achieve the same thing.  
Worse, there are times when using `=` can have undesirable effects on your code b/c it may evaluate instead of assign or vise versa.  

And don't forget about `%%` meaning different things.  
In classic syntax, it means "use this as a variable".  
In expression syntax, it means "dereference this variable", or, in other words, use the contents of the variable as the variable name.  

    ; Classic
    text = Hello`, world!
    MsgBox, %text%

    ; Expression
    text := "Hello, world!"
    MsgBox, % text

All the "classic syntax" stuff is gone in v2.  
Unlearn it.  
v2 is expression syntax only.  
What does that mean?  
Pretty much the same rules as from v1 for expressions. But lets recap:

* `:=` is for assigning values and `=` is used for comparison/checking if things are equal to each other.  

        toggle := 1                ; Assign 1 to toggle
        if (toggle = 1)            ; If toggle is equal to 1
            MsgBox('Hello!')       ; Say hello
        else my_msg := 'Goodbye!'
        MsgBox(my_msg)

* All strings (text) must have quotes around them.  
  v2 Bonus: You can use single or double quotes for strings.  
  Use single quotes. Your other pinky will thank you.  
  Variables and numbers do not have quotes around them.

        word1 := 'Hello'          ; Single quotes
        word2 := "World!"         ; Double quotes
        MsgBox(word1 " " word2)   ; " "  puts a space between the words

  "But what if I need to use single quotes in the text?"  
  Then use double quotes around the string.  

        str := "v2 isn't hard to learn."

  "What if I need both?"  
  Then use a backtick to escape the quotation marks so AHK knows it's a literal quote and not the end of the string. Works with both quote types.  

        ; Notice the backtick in the word isn't
        ; This tells AHK you want a literal single quote and not end the string
        str := 'GroggyOtter says, "v2 isn`'t hard to learn."'

  Escapes are not new to v2. v1 has them as well. However, v2 added the ability to escape quotes so we don't have to use double quotes for literal quotes anymore `""`.  
  Anyone else worth with command line and paths in v1 and end up with a huge blob of double quotes? Not a problem in v2.

* Numbers and variables are typed normally.  

        pi := 3.14159
        MsgBox('The value of pi is: ' pi)

  You can put numbers in strings but then it's technically a string of number characters.  
  But AHK will still do math with it b/c it still converts string to pure numbers when appropriate.

        num1 := 5
        num2 := '4'
        sum := num1 + num2
        MsgBox(sum)

* `%%` are used to "dereference" things.  
  This means "use what's INSIDE the variable as the variable name".  
  Remember that , variables are typed normally and don't use `%%` around them anymore.

        ; Dereferencing example
        var := 'Hello'
        banana := 'var'
        MsgBox(%banana%)  ; Is the same as MsgBox(var)

   This is one of those things where you don't use until you realize you need it. So it's good to know about it.

# - Hotkeys and hotstrings use curly braces because they are functions.

A theme you're going to see in v2 is "everything is a function or an object".  
In v2, hotkeys and hotstrings are now a special way to make a function.  
That's why we need the curly braces. Just like making a normal function:

    ; A normal function
    max_restore() {
        state := WinGetMinMax('A')  ; Get the min/max state of the window
        if (state = 1)              ; If maximized
            WinRestore('A')         ;   Restore the window
        else if (state = 0)         ; else if state is normal
            WinMaximize('A')        ;   Maximize window
    }

    ; A hotkey
    F1:: {
        state := WinGetMinMax('A')  ; Get the min/max state of the window
        if (state = 1)              ; If maximized
            WinRestore('A')         ;   Restore the window
        else if (state = 0)         ; else if state is normal
            WinMaximize('A')        ;   Maximize window
    }

    ; A hotstring
    ::/max_restore:: {
        state := WinGetMinMax('A')  ; Get the min/max state of the window
        if (state = 1)              ; If maximized
            WinRestore('A')         ;   Restore the window
        else if (state = 0)         ; else if state is normal
            WinMaximize('A')        ;   Maximize window
    }

Notice how they're pretty much identical?  

You can even name them.

Inline hotkeys and hotstrings are still allowed and do not require curly braces.  
This is the same as in v1 where they don't require a return statement. It's just `hotkey::Do_Something()`

    F1::WinMaximize('A')
    :x:/winmax::WinMaximize('A')

* No global labels or gosub.

Global labels were removed b/c they were pretty much lesser-functions that served no real value.  
GoSub was also removed b/c it's an antiquated way of programming and people shouldn't be using label jumps to write code. It's messy, inefficient, and becomes increasingly difficult to maintain.  

Lables do still exist in v2, but their purpose is for jumping around inside of loops.  
If you don't need to do jumps while looping through stuff, you don't need labels.  
Use your functions (or better yet, learn to use classes! They're really powerful and make coding easier.)  

# -Utilize Visual Studio + THQBY's AHK v2 addon!!

This one is huge.  
If you use [Visual Studio Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/download) and you [install THQBY's AHK v2 addon](https://i.imgur.com/tITgSnx.png), your AHK v2 coding life will become so much easier.  
