# - Errors are a good thing. Stop thinking they're bad.

One complaint I see some people make is "v2 errors more often!"  
This isn't exactly true or fair.  
What's more accurate is "v1 lets you get away with things that it shouldn't."  

I think people have an issue with errors b/c they think the interpreter is telling them they're stupid.  
Do NOT think like that.  
We're humans. We error ALL THE TIME. I'm extremely proficient in v2 and I easily make errors b/c we can't track things 100% of the time with 100% accuracy. It's just not feasible. It's not human-like.  
v2 is more strict about things and it seems like it errors out more b/c it's trying to make your code more stable and more reliable.  

Example: In v1, if you try to work with a window and it doesn't exist, AHK just tries and if it fails, that's that. It doesn't tell you one way or the other.  
In v2, if you try to work with a window that doesn't exist, you get an error because the window you requested isn't there. That IS an error and AHK is notifying you about it.  
["v2 is too strict!!! I'm going back to v1!!"](https://media4.giphy.com/media/v1.Y2lkPTc5MGI3NjExMTl3M2d5eXR0Y3V5MTEzejdzZzI2ODIzcmJvNXI1dnEwcmgwYzIydiZlcD12MV9pbnRlcm5hbF9naWZfYnlfaWQmY3Q9Zw/EpyCyEf0jwSCBa8hOA/giphy.webp)  
v2 isn't being more difficult. It's being more thorough.  
If you want the code to work conditionally, then you need to check for the window to exist before you act on it.  

Errors show you problems or shortcomings in the code and are there to help you write better, more stable code.  
In no way are errors a reflection of your smartness or stupidity.  

# - Callbacks

What even is a callback??  
It's a function that gets called by something later on.  
The function gets "called back" by something.  

A prime example of callbacks are GUI events.  
If you make a GUI button and you want a function to run when you click it, then you make a that function has to be able to take in the correct amount of parameters.  
In our button click example, the callback function needs to accept 2 parameters.  

    ; Example of a callback
    goo := Gui()
    con := goo.AddButton(, 'Hello')
    goo.Show()

    ; This is a callback
    ; It's just a function that accepts some pre-defined parameters
    ; Button click events need 2 parameters.
    ; The first for a reference to the button
    ; The second is extra info, which isn't really used with button click but still needs to be there.
    on_click(btn_control, info) {
        ; The code to run when the button is clicked
        MsgBox('The button text is: ' btn_control.text)
    }


